        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n\r\n> BTW thanks for this actually super cool and awesome plugin; I've managed to replace like 15+ plugins with just this one!\r\n\r\nThat is a very happy and exactly the intended outcome!\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\n> I think it would be better if instead the highlighting features be removed, because they can already be done with vanilla (Neo)Vim options: by using `listchars`'s `trail` setting.\r\n\r\nYou are correct that the main functionality of  'mini.trailspace' is to highlight trailing whitepsace with a special dedicated highlight group. But it does that a little bit smarter as `trail` from `listchars`:\r\n- It allows using separate highlight group instead of built-in `Whitespace` (which is used for many other things).\r\n- It highlights only in Normal mode and stops in Insert mode, while with `listchars` it is shown in Insert mode. It was the main reason for 'mini.trailspace' because that `trail` character appearing after typing every space at the end of line was a bit annoying.\r\n\r\n> In addition to that, I'd also think it would be better if, similar to [tidy.nvim](https://github.com/mcauley-penney/tidy.nvim), there would be an option to automatically set up an autocommand to format the file using the Lua functions upon writing the buffer. A command (and maybe keybind) to toggle between this feature being enabled and doing it manually.\r\n\r\nWriting an autocommand which calls [`trim()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/eaf0e040362fa0c20db1a7f0cccda9099b6b0362/doc/mini-trailspace.txt#L81) and [`trim_last_lines()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/eaf0e040362fa0c20db1a7f0cccda9099b6b0362/doc/mini-trailspace.txt#L86) on desired event should be pretty straightforward call to `vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd()`. Toggling feature can be implemented by having a global user-level flag on which formatting is conditioned.\r\n\r\nClosing as not planned.",
